    <section id="interdisciplinary-path">
      <h2>An interdisciplinary path</h2>

      <p>
        My academic and professional trajectory has developed across law,
        criminology, environmental science, public safety, and marine ecology.
        This combination shapes how I approach environmental crime and harm:
        not only as legal questions, but also as ecological and social processes.
      </p>

      <div class="extras-timeline">

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <p class="timeline-label">Environmental science</p>

          <h3>Environmental microbiology and laboratory analysis</h3>

          <p>
            My initial scientific training was in environmental health.
            I subsequently worked in environmental microbiology, applying
            laboratory methods to the analysis of water, foodborne pathogens,
            and environmental samples.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <p class="timeline-label">Public service</p>

          <h3>Security, policing, and local administration</h3>

          <p>
            My professional experience in public administration and public
            safety provided direct contact with regulatory enforcement,
            environmental inspection, noise pollution, emergencies,
            and the practical operation of public institutions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <p class="timeline-label">Law and criminology</p>

          <h3>Environmental criminal law and green criminology</h3>

          <p>
            My legal and criminological research brought these earlier
            experiences together through the study of environmental crime,
            ecological harm, criminal policy, biodiversity protection,
            and environmental justice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <p class="timeline-label">Marine ecology</p>

          <h3>Connecting legal categories with ecological evidence</h3>

          <p>
            My current interdisciplinary work examines how ecological
            knowledge and marine evidence can contribute to legal and
            criminological assessments of damage affecting aquatic ecosystems.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

